problem_statement:
[Feature] Support Dagger multibinding for Map (IntoMap)
## Summary

Add a new `@AutoBindsIntoMap` annotation that generates `@Binds @IntoMap` modules, completing the multibinding support alongside the existing `@AutoBindsIntoSet`. The map key annotation is placed directly on the class, mirroring how Dagger itself uses `@IntoMap` with a separate key annotation.

## Problem

Dagger's `@IntoMap` multibinding lets you build a `Map<K, V>` where each entry is contributed by a separate class. This is a common pattern for plugin registries. Today each entry requires a manual module:

```kotlin
@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
internal interface ServiceModule {
  @Binds @IntoMap @ClassKey(HomeService::class)
  fun bindHome(impl: HomeService): Service

  @Binds @IntoMap @ClassKey(ProfileService::class)
  fun bindProfile(impl: ServiceViewModel): Service
  // every new Service means editing this file
}
```

There is no way to express this with `@AutoBinds` or `@AutoBindsIntoSet` today.

## Proposed API

### New annotation: `@AutoBindsIntoMap`

```kotlin
@Target(AnnotationTarget.CLASS)
@Retention(AnnotationRetention.SOURCE)
annotation class AutoBindsIntoMap(
    val installIn: HiltComponent = HiltComponent.Unspecified,
    val bindTo: Array<KClass<*>> = [],
)
```

The map key is **not** a parameter of `@AutoBindsIntoMap`. Instead, the user places a Dagger map key annotation directly on the class, exactly as they would when writing the module by hand. The processor detects any annotation that is itself meta-annotated with `@dagger.MapKey` and forwards it to the generated function. 

This mirrors Dagger's own design and requires no new concepts to learn.

### Example: `@ClassKey`

```kotlin
@AutoBindsIntoMap
@ClassKey(HomeService::class)
class HomeService @Inject constructor() : Service()

@AutoBindsIntoMap
@ClassKey(ProfileService::class)
class ProfileService @Inject constructor() : Service()
```

**Generated:**

```kotlin
@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
internal interface HomeServiceModule {
    @Binds
    @IntoMap
    @ClassKey(HomeService::class)
    fun bindToService(impl: HomeService): Service
}

@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
internal interface ProfileServiceModule {
    @Binds
    @IntoMap
    @ClassKey(ProfileService::class)
    fun bindToService(impl: ProfileService): Service
}
```

**Injection site (unchanged):**

```kotlin
class ServiceFactory @Inject constructor(
    private val creators: Map<Class<*>, @JvmSuppressWildcards Provider<Service>>,
)
```

### Example: `@StringKey` (plugin / strategy registry)

```kotlin
@AutoBindsIntoMap
@StringKey("json")
class JsonSerializer @Inject constructor() : Serializer

@AutoBindsIntoMap
@StringKey("xml")
class XmlSerializer @Inject constructor() : Serializer
```

**Generated:**

```kotlin
@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
internal interface JsonSerializerModule {
 @Binds
 @IntoMap
 @StringKey("json")
 fun bindToSerializer(impl: JsonSerializer): Serializer
}

// ... the same for XmlSerializer ...
```

**Injection site:**

```kotlin
class SerializerRegistry @Inject constructor(
 private val serializers: Map<String, @JvmSuppressWildcards Serializer>,
)
```

### Example: custom `@MapKey`

```kotlin
// Custom map key defined once
@MapKey
annotation class ScreenKey(val value: KClass<out Screen>)

// Each screen contributes itself to the map
@AutoBindsIntoMap
@ScreenKey(HomeScreen::class)
class HomePresenter @Inject constructor() : Presenter

@AutoBindsIntoMap
@ScreenKey(SettingsScreen::class)
class SettingsPresenter @Inject constructor() : Presenter
```

**Generated:**

```kotlin
@Module
@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)
internal interface HomePresenterModule {
 @Binds
 @IntoMap
 @ScreenKey(HomeScreen::class)
 fun bindToPresenter(impl: HomePresenter): Presenter
}
```

### Selecting the value type with bindTo

If the class has multiple supertypes, `bindTo` selects which one becomes the map
value type. Without `bindTo`, all direct supertypes are bound (one `@Binds @IntoMap`
per supertype, each contributing to a separate map).

```kotlin
interface Presenter
interface Closeable

// contributes only to Map<..., Presenter>, not Map<..., Closeable>
@AutoBindsIntoMap(bindTo = [Presenter::class])
@StringKey("home")
class HomePresenter @Inject constructor() : Presenter, Closeable
```

### Combining with @AutoBinds and @AutoBindsIntoSet

`@AutoBindsIntoMap` can be combined with `@AutoBinds` or `@AutoBindsIntoSet` on the
same class, generating separate modules for each annotation:

```kotlin
@AutoBinds            // direct binding: inject HomePresenter as Presenter
@AutoBindsIntoMap     // also contribute to Map<String, Presenter>
@StringKey("home")
class HomePresenter @Inject constructor() : Presenter
```

## Compile-time validation

The processor should emit errors for the following:

| Violation | Error message |
|---|---|
| No map key annotation on the class | `@AutoBindsIntoMap requires a map key annotation (e.g. @StringKey, @ClassKey) on 'HomePresenter'` |
| More than one map key annotation | `'HomePresenter' has multiple map key annotations; only one is allowed` |
| `bindTo` type is not a supertype | *(same error as in `@AutoBindsIntoSet`)* |

[HIDDEN]

### Additional contract requirements

This addendum clarifies the public contract for the `@AutoBindsIntoMap`
feature. Where it differs from the base problem statement, this addendum
governs.

## Annotation shape

The feature adds a public annotation `AutoBindsIntoMap` that may be placed on a
concrete class or meta-placed on another annotation (so users can define
annotation aliases), and it is visible at least at the class-file (binary)
level. It declares exactly two parameters, both optional:

- `installIn`: selects the Hilt component the generated module is installed in.
  When omitted, the component is auto-detected from the scope annotation on the
  annotated class, and falls back to the singleton component for unscoped
  classes.
- `bindTo`: an optional list of supertypes to contribute the annotated class
  into. When omitted, the class is contributed to a map of each of its direct
  supertypes.

## Map keys

- The map key is not a parameter of the annotation. The user places any
  annotation that is itself meta-annotated with Dagger's `@MapKey` (built-in
  keys such as `@StringKey` or `@ClassKey`, or a user-defined `@MapKey`
  annotation) directly on the annotated class, and that key annotation is
  forwarded to the generated `@Binds @IntoMap` binding function.
- If no `@MapKey`-meta-annotated annotation is present on the class, it is a
  successful fallback (not an error): the generated function is annotated with
  `@ClassKey` whose value is the annotated class itself, so the contribution
  lands in a `Map<Class<*>, ...>`. This fallback overrides the no-map-key
  diagnostic row in the base problem statement.
- Having more than one `@MapKey`-meta-annotated annotation on the same class is
  a compile-time error.

## Generated bindings

For each annotated class, the feature generates a Hilt module annotated with
`@Module` and `@InstallIn` for the resolved component. It contains one
`@Binds @IntoMap` binding for each targeted supertype. Each binding carries the
resolved map key annotation, accepts the annotated class, and returns the
targeted supertype.

The contract does not prescribe generated file names, imports, formatting, or
Kotlin visibility modifiers.

## Combining with other annotations

`@AutoBindsIntoMap` may be combined with `@AutoBinds` and/or
`@AutoBindsIntoSet` on the same class. Each annotation produces its own
separate generated module with the corresponding binding semantics.

## Validation

The following are compile-time errors:

- More than one `@MapKey`-meta-annotated annotation on the annotated class.
- A `bindTo` entry that is not a transitive supertype of the annotated class;
  the diagnostic identifies the invalid type and states that it is not a
  supertype.

