problem_statement:
Crossing a zone boundary makes a player invisible to everyone, including themselves: the destination zone delivers nothing
**Version:** `ghcr.io/widgrensit/asobi_lua:latest`, revision
`719bec977272155bc764455c7db77a2de4d3e8a1`, built 2026-08-01T14:41Z — reports
`asobi-0.47.6`. That is the build carrying #248's re-home fix plus the Lua-side
key normalisation from asobi_lua#113.

Regression introduced with the re-home path. Interest management itself now
works correctly — this is about what happens at the moment of the crossing.

## What is fixed

Confirmed on this build, with the walking-away repro from #248: a player 11
zones from a stationary subscriber is filtered out, and so are the props of
their zone.

```
both spawned in zone 1000; Ada receives Bob: true
  t+3s  Bob 2 zones away  Ada receives him: no
  t+12s Bob 9 zones away  Ada receives him: no
Bob is 11 zones from Ada with view_radius = 1
Ada still receives Bob:   false
props from >2 zones away: 0
```

## What broke

A player who walks across **one** zone boundary stops being delivered by the
zone they walked into — to anyone. They do not see their own entity, the
neighbour whose interest ring still covers that zone stops seeing them, and a
second player who walks into the same zone sees neither them nor themselves.

It is not a dead socket: the crossing player keeps receiving `world.tick` the
whole time, because they are still subscribed to the zone they came from. Only
the destination zone contributes nothing.

It does not resolve on its own. Walking back into the spawn zone restores
everything immediately.

## Reproduction

`zone-crossing-blackout.mjs` (attached below), three guest clients, walking at
12 units/s with inputs at 15 Hz. Zones are 16 units, so one crossing takes about
1.3 seconds — well inside the #266 hysteresis and rate limit.

```
$ node zone-crossing-blackout.mjs
all three spawned in zone 1000,1000
ok    everyone sees everyone in the spawn zone  -- Ada holds 3, Bob holds 3

Bob crossed into zone 1001; Ada is in 1000 -- 1 apart, inside view_radius
ok    Bob still receives world.tick  -- 19 -> 59
FAIL  Bob sees his own entity  -- holds 2 entities
FAIL  Ada still sees Bob

Cara walked into zone 1001 too, standing next to Bob
FAIL  Cara sees Bob
FAIL  Cara sees herself
FAIL  Bob sees Cara
     entities held -- Bob: 1, Cara: 1
FAIL  Bob's own entity comes back after 15 s of standing there  -- ticks 98 -> 196 (still flowing), entities 1
ok    walking back into the spawn zone restores him  -- entities 2, ticks 234

6 check(s) failed -- players go dark on a zone crossing
```

Two players standing next to each other in zone 1001, each holding exactly one
entity — the third player, still back in the spawn zone.

The world script is deliberately inert, so nothing here depends on what a game
does per tick:

```lua
-- lua/minimal.lua, registered as { minimal = "minimal.lua" } in lua/config.lua,
-- which also needs guest_auth = true
game_type   = "world"
match_size  = 1
max_players = 16
grid_size   = 2000
zone_size   = 16
view_radius = 1
lazy_zones  = true

local SPAWN = grid_size * zone_size / 2

function init(config) return {} end
function generate_world(seed, config) return {} end
function spawn_position(player_id, state) return { x = SPAWN, y = SPAWN } end
function join(player_id, state) return state end
function leave(player_id, state) return state end
function post_tick(tick_n, state) return state end
function zone_tick(entities, zone_state) return entities, zone_state end

function handle_input(player_id, input, entities)
    if not input or input.kind ~= "move" then return entities end
    local e = entities[player_id] or {}
    e.type = "player"
    e.x = tonumber(input.x) or SPAWN
    e.y = tonumber(input.y) or SPAWN
    e.seq = tonumber(input.seq) or 0
    e.name = input.name or e.name
    entities[player_id] = e
    return entities
end
```

Also reproduced on a full game script with props and per-tick work, with the
same result, so the minimal script is not hiding anything: after the crossing
the client held 0 entities and 0 props for as long as it stayed there.

[HIDDEN]
## Expected

After a crossing, the destination zone delivers its contents to the player who
entered it and to every subscriber whose interest ring covers it — the same way
the spawn zone does at join.

Two things have to line up for that: the crossing player must end up subscribed
to the zone they moved into even when it was already in their ring, and a zone
coming into existence has to pick up the subscribers whose rings already cover
it (or those rings have to be re-resolved when it loads).

## Repro script

<details>
<summary><code>zone-crossing-blackout.mjs</code></summary>

```js
// Repro: crossing a single zone boundary drops a player out of the world.
//
// Self-contained. Node 22+ (built-in WebSocket and fetch), no deps:
//
//     node zone-crossing-blackout.mjs [host:port]
//
// Needs guest_auth = true, a >= 32-byte ASOBI_GUEST_VERIFIER_PEPPER, and a
// world mode (default "minimal", override with MODE=) running the minimal
// script from the issue this ships with. Override ZONE_SIZE/SPAWN to match a
// different world.

import crypto from "node:crypto";

const HOST = process.argv[2] || "localhost:8080";
const MODE = process.env.MODE || "minimal";
const ZONE = Number(process.env.ZONE_SIZE || 16);
const SPAWN = Number(process.env.SPAWN || 16000);

const SPEED = 12;   // units per second: a normal walking client
const HZ = 15;      // input rate

const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
const b64 = (n) => crypto.randomBytes(n).toString("base64");
const zoneOf = (v) => Math.floor(v / ZONE);

class Client {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name; this.entities = new Map(); this.joined = false;
    this.placed = false; this.ticks = 0; this.cid = 0; this.seq = 0;
    this.x = SPAWN; this.y = SPAWN;
  }

  async start() {
    const res = await fetch(`http://${HOST}/api/v1/auth/guest`, {
      method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ device_id: b64(16), device_secret: b64(32) }),
    });
    const auth = await res.json();
    if (!auth.access_token) throw new Error(`${this.name}: auth ${res.status} ${JSON.stringify(auth)}`);
    this.playerId = auth.player_id;
    this.ws = new WebSocket(`ws://${HOST}/ws`);
    await new Promise((ok, fail) => { this.ws.onopen = ok; this.ws.onerror = fail; });
    this.ws.onmessage = (e) => this.onMessage(JSON.parse(e.data));
    this.send("session.connect", { token: auth.access_token });
    for (let i = 0; i < 100 && !this.placed; i++) await sleep(100);
    if (!this.placed) throw new Error(`${this.name}: no spawn placement`);
  }

  send(type, payload) { this.ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type, payload, cid: String(++this.cid) })); }

  onMessage({ type, payload = {} }) {
    if (type === "session.connected") this.send("world.find_or_create", { mode: MODE });
    else if (type === "world.joined") this.joined = true;
    else if (type === "world.tick") {
      this.ticks++;
      for (const u of payload.updates || []) {
        if (u.op === "r") { this.entities.delete(u.id); continue; }
        const prev = u.op === "u" ? this.entities.get(u.id) || {} : {};
        this.entities.set(u.id, { ...prev, ...u });
        if (u.id === this.playerId && !this.placed) this.placed = true;
      }
    } else if (type.startsWith("world.") && !this.joined && payload.world_id) {
      this.send("world.join", { world_id: payload.world_id });
    }
  }

  report() {
    this.ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: "world.input",
      payload: { kind: "move", x: this.x, y: this.y, yaw: 0, name: this.name, seq: ++this.seq } }));
  }

  async walkTo(x) {
    const dir = Math.sign(x - this.x);
    while (dir > 0 ? this.x < x : this.x > x) {
      this.x += dir * SPEED / HZ;
      await sleep(1000 / HZ);
    }
  }
}

let failures = 0;
const check = (label, ok, detail = "") => {
  console.log(`${ok ? "ok  " : "FAIL"}  ${label}${detail ? "  -- " + detail : ""}`);
  if (!ok) failures++;
};

const ada = new Client("Ada"), bob = new Client("Bob"), cara = new Client("Cara");
