problem_statement:
Leaving a zone's interest ring never removes its entities: players you walk away from stay on screen forever, frozen

**Version:** asobi `3fa21157` (the #275 fix) built into an asobi_lua image from
`main` — reports `asobi-0.47.8`.

Follow-up to #248 and #275. Both are fixed: a crossing now re-homes the player,
the destination zone delivers, and a player far away stops being *updated*. What
is missing is the other half — the client is never told to drop what it already
has.

## Summary

`asobi_zone` hands a new subscriber the zone's full contents:

```erlang
%% src/world/asobi_zone.erl:493, in subscribe_new/3
Snapshot = [E#{~"op" => ~"a", ~"id" => Id} || {Id, E} <- maps:to_list(Entities)],
PlayerPid ! {asobi_message, {zone_delta, 0, Snapshot}}
```

Unsubscribing sends nothing back:

```erlang
%% src/world/asobi_zone.erl:397
handle_cast({unsubscribe, PlayerId}, #{subscribers := Subs} = State) ->
    case maps:get(PlayerId, Subs, undefined) of
        undefined ->
            {noreply, State};
        {_Pid, MonRef} ->
            demonitor(MonRef, [flush]),
            {noreply, State#{subscribers => maps:remove(PlayerId, Subs)}}
    end;
```

So a client that walks out of a zone's ring keeps every entity that zone gave
it, with no `r` op ever arriving and no further updates. They freeze at their
last known state and stay there for the rest of the session.

## Reproduction

`stale-entities-on-unsubscribe.mjs` (below). Two guests, walking at 12 units/s
with inputs at 15 Hz, 16-unit zones, `view_radius = 1`. Bob walks three zones
east; Ada does not move.

```
$ node stale-entities-on-unsubscribe.mjs
ok    they see each other in the spawn zone

Bob is in zone 1003, Ada in 1000 -- 3 apart, outside view_radius = 1
ok    Ada stopped receiving Bob  -- his entity really moved zones, so the old zone sent a removal
FAIL  Bob stopped receiving Ada  -- still holds her at x=16000 seq=80
      (props still held from zones he left: 0)

Ada walked to y=16040; Bob's copy of her says y=16000, seq=80 (was seq=80)
FAIL  and what he holds is at least live, not a frozen ghost  -- her position never changes again for him

2 check(s) failed -- entities of a zone that left the ring are never removed
```

The asymmetry in that output is the whole story:

- **Ada correctly loses Bob.** His entity physically moved zones during the
  crossing, so the zone she is subscribed to emitted a real removal for it.
- **Bob never loses Ada.** She stayed where she was; all that ended was his
  subscription — and that path emits nothing. Her `seq` is 80 before and after
  she walks 40 units, so what he holds is not a lagging copy, it is a dead one.

The world script is inert (`zone_tick` returns the entity map unchanged), so
nothing here depends on game logic:

```lua
-- lua/minimal.lua, registered as { minimal = "minimal.lua" } in lua/config.lua,
-- which also needs guest_auth = true
game_type   = "world"
match_size  = 1
max_players = 16
grid_size   = 2000
zone_size   = 16
view_radius = 1
lazy_zones  = true

local SPAWN = grid_size * zone_size / 2

function init(config) return {} end
function generate_world(seed, config) return {} end
function spawn_position(player_id, state) return { x = SPAWN, y = SPAWN } end
function join(player_id, state) return state end
function leave(player_id, state) return state end
function post_tick(tick_n, state) return state end
function zone_tick(entities, zone_state) return entities, zone_state end

function handle_input(player_id, input, entities)
    if not input or input.kind ~= "move" then return entities end
    local e = entities[player_id] or {}
    e.type = "player"
    e.x = tonumber(input.x) or SPAWN
    e.y = tonumber(input.y) or SPAWN
    e.seq = tonumber(input.seq) or 0
    e.name = input.name or e.name
    entities[player_id] = e
    return entities
end
```

## Why it can look fine in a real game

A game script that despawns entities when their zone drops out of its own
interest set produces genuine `r` ops, and those do clean up the client — so
props appear to behave. That masks the defect rather than avoiding it: anything
the script leaves alive in the zone it walked away from — other players above
all, and any NPC — sticks forever. In our game the visible result is a trail of
motionless duplicates of every player we have ever walked past.

There is no client-side signal to work around it with either. `world.tick`
carries no zone identity, so a client cannot tell "this entity belongs to a zone
I just left" from "this entity stopped changing". The only workaround is for the
client to re-derive the ring itself from entity coordinates and drop what falls
outside — which is exactly the bookkeeping interest management exists to remove.

## Expected

Unsubscribing should mirror subscribing: as `subscribe_new/3` sends `a` for
every entity in the zone, [HIDDEN] — so that ending a subscription
leaves the client with a correct view rather than a stale one.

## Repro script

<details>
<summary><code>stale-entities-on-unsubscribe.mjs</code></summary>

```js
// Repro: leaving a zone's interest ring never removes that zone's entities
// from the client, so they stay forever, frozen.
//
// Self-contained. Node 22+ (built-in WebSocket and fetch), no deps:
//
//     node stale-entities-on-unsubscribe.mjs [host:port]
//
// Needs guest_auth = true, a >= 32-byte ASOBI_GUEST_VERIFIER_PEPPER, and a
// world mode (default "minimal", override with MODE=) keeping a `player`
// entity per client. The issue this ships with has a minimal script.

import crypto from "node:crypto";

const HOST = process.argv[2] || "localhost:8080";
const MODE = process.env.MODE || "minimal";
const ZONE = Number(process.env.ZONE_SIZE || 16);
const SPAWN = Number(process.env.SPAWN || 16000);
const SPEED = 12, HZ = 15;

const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
const b64 = (n) => crypto.randomBytes(n).toString("base64");
const zoneOf = (v) => Math.floor(v / ZONE);

class Client {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name; this.entities = new Map(); this.removals = 0;
    this.joined = false; this.placed = false; this.cid = 0; this.seq = 0;
    this.x = SPAWN; this.y = SPAWN;
  }
  async start() {
    const res = await fetch(`http://${HOST}/api/v1/auth/guest`, {
      method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
